  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nMid level closed low will develop this morning over western\nPennsylvania with two substantial short waves rotating through the\nflow. Primary energy from these two disturbances will largely stay\nwest and south of the region. So mid level forcing will be minimal.\nLow level cold air advection will persist this morning and then\nbecome more neutral. \n\nRemaining showers will continue to off to the south early this\nmorning. Additional spotty showers will be possible through the day \nwith the exception of central Ohio where there is a better chance of \nmore concentrated activity, although even with that QPF is light.\n\nBelow normal temperatures will persist through the weekend.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nMid level long wave trough will move east to start the week which\nwill allow heights to rise. There will be a system opening up as it \npass south of the area early in the week, but this will be far enough\nsouth to have no impact other than perhaps temporarily delaying \nfurther height rises. Mid level ridge axis will eventually spread \nover the area on Wednesday and then slide southeast on Thursday. \nSurface high pressure will prevail through the week with southerly \nflow returning Wednesday into Thursday. \n\nThis will result in quiescent conditions and a warming trend.\nTemperatures will rise back above normal by Wednesday.",
